''lier'', ''asolier'', also ''asoldre'', ''assoldre'', ''assoudre'', etc., < L. ''absolvere'', absolve, loosen: see ''absolve'', of which ''assoil'' is thus a doublet.] '''1†.''' To solve; clear up.
{{smaller block|To ''assoil'' this seeming difficulty.
{{right|''Waterland'', Scripture Vindicated, iii. 63.}}}}
'''2.''' To release; set free; acquit; pardon; absolve. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|At my own tribunal stand ''assoil’d.'' {{right|''Tuke.''}}
<poem>To some bishop we will wend,
Of all the sins that we have done,
To be ''assoiled'' at his hand. ''Percy’s Reliques.''</poem>}}
'''3†.''' To remove; dispel.
{{smaller block|Seeking him that should her paine ''assoyle.''
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. v. 30.}}}}

'''assoil<sup>2</sup>†''' (a-soil′), ''v. t.'' [< ''as-''<sup>1</sup> + ''soil''<sup>1</sup>.] To soil; stain.
{{smaller block|<poem>Whate’er he be
Can with unthankfulness ''assoil'' me, let him
Dig out mine eyes, and sing my name in verse.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher (and another)'', Queen of Corinth, iii. 1.}}}}

'''assoilment†''' (a-soil′ment), ''n.'' [< ''assoil''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ment.''] The act of assoiling; absolution. ''More.''

'''assoilyie, assoilzie''' (a-soil′yē), ''v. t.'' Scotch forms of ''assoil''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|God ''assoilzie'' him for the sin of bloodshed.
{{right|''Scott'', Ivanhoe, II. vi.}}}}

'''assonance''' (as′ọ̄-nans), ''n.'' [< F. ''assonance'' (= Sp. ''asonancia'' = Pg. ''assonancia''), < ''assonant'': see ''assonant'', ''a.''] '''1.''' Resemblance of sounds.
{{smaller block|The disagreeable ''assonance'' of “sheath” and “sheathed.”
{{right|''Steevens.''}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|The combination of cadenced sentences with antithetical alliteration, intersprinkled with ''assonances'' of every kind and their inevitable offspring, the uncalled-for pun, was by him [Lyly] first introduced into English prose.
{{right|''A. W. Ward'', Eng. Dram. Lit., I. 157.}}}}
{{smaller block|Homer, like Dante and Shakespeare, like all who really command language, seems fond of playing with ''assonances''.
{{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 327.}}}}
Specifically—'''2.''' In ''pros.'', a species of imperfect rime, or rather a substitute for rime, especially common in Spanish poetry, consisting in using the same vowel-sound with different consonants, and requiring the use of the same vowels in the assonant words from the last accented vowel to the end of the word: thus, ''man'' and ''hat'', ''penitent'' and ''reticence'', are examples of assonance in English.
{{smaller block|There are some traces of the employment of rhyme and ''assonance'' in mere popular literature at a very remote period.
{{right|''G. P. Marsh'', Lects. on Eng. Lang., p. 505.}}}}
'''3.''' Agreement or harmony of things. [Rare.] ='''Syn.''' ''Paronomasia'', etc. See ''pun''.

'''assonanced''' (as′ọ̄-nanst), ''a.'' [< ''assonance'' + -''ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Characterized by assonance; assonant.
{{smaller block|The lines are, in the earlier examples, ''assonanced'',—that is to say, the vowel sound of the last syllables is identical, but the consonants need not agree.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', IX. 638.}}}}

'''assonant''' (as′ọ̄-na̤nt), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< F. ''assonant'' (= Sp. ''asonante'' = Pg. ''assonante''), < L. ''assonan(t-)s'', ppr. of ''assonare'', sound to, respond to: see ''assonate'' and ''sonant''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Having a resemblance of articulate sounds.
{{smaller block|Landor’s blank verse . . . is . . . terse, yet fluent, ''assonant'', harmonious.
{{right|''Stedman'', Vict. Poets, p. 46.}}}}
